.TH CGMINESWEEP 6 "November 2026" "cggames 1.4.0"
.SH NAME
cgminesweep \- clear the minefield
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cgminesweep
.RI [ "toolkit options" ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cgminesweep
hides mines under the squares of a field.
Uncover every square that holds no mine to win; uncovering a mine loses
the game.
A square's number tells how many of the eight squares round it hold
mines.
.PP
The left button uncovers a square.
The first square uncovered never holds a mine: one there moves to the
first free square from the top left, as in Windows' Minesweeper.
The right button puts a flag on a square; pressed again, it puts a
question mark there instead, and then takes it away.
.B Settings \(-> Marks (?)
turns the question marks off, so that the right button only puts on
and takes off flags.
A question mark doesn't count as a flag, and the square can still be
uncovered.
The middle button, both left and right together, or Shift with the left
button, on a number whose mines are all flagged, uncovers the squares
round it.
The face starts a new game.
.PP
The figures at the top left count the mines not yet flagged, and those
at the top right the seconds, from the first click.
While a game runs, the field is hidden when the window loses the focus,
until
.B Press to resume
is pressed.
.PP
The field is Beginner (8 by 8, 10 mines), Intermediate (16 by 16, 40
mines), Expert (30 by 16, 99 mines) or Custom, set in the preferences
with the size of the squares.
The ten best times are kept for each fixed size.
A custom field's score is the share of its squares that are mines, in
per cent, for each second the game took, times 100; the ten highest
are kept.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/cggames/cgminesweep.conf
The preferences.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/cggames/cgminesweep.scores
The best scores.
.PP
Without the variables,
.I ~/.config
and
.I ~/.local/share
are used.
.SH AUTHORS
cgminesweep is GNOME Mines (gnomine) from gnome-games 1.4, ported to
CGTK.
Szekeres Istvan (Pista) wrote GNOME Mines, HoraPe added the scores, and
Donnie Barnes made the squares resizable.
Its faces were Tuomas Kuosmanen's (tigert); the face, the figures and
the squares are now drawn by the program, after Windows' Minesweeper.
